  "nl_text": "The database schema \"rschema_0121\" contains eight tables, each representing a different entity or relationship in the system. The tables are:\n1. \"user\" - This table stores information about users, including their user ID, username, full name, email, password hash, registration date, and last login date. The primary key is the user ID.\n2. \"authenticated_user\" - This table stores information about authenticated users, including their user ID, authentication level, signature, issuer, and authentication time. The primary key is the user ID, and there is a foreign key constraint on the user ID that references the \"user\" table.\n3. \"service\" - This table stores information about services, including their service ID, service name, service type, service URL, configuration data, last heartbeat time, monitoring status, signature, and issuer. The primary key is the service ID.\n4. \"worker_node\" - This table stores information about worker nodes, including their node ID, node name, status, service ID, operating system, architecture, last heartbeat time, signature, and issuer. The primary key is the node ID, and there is a foreign key constraint on the service ID that references the \"service\" table.\n5. \"authenticated_consumer\" - This table stores information about authenticated consumers, including their consumer ID, user ID, name, description, type, configuration data, permissions, signature, and issuer. The primary key is the user ID and consumer ID, and there is a foreign key constraint on the user ID that references the \"user\" table.\n6. \"user_migration\" - This table stores information about user migrations, including their migration ID, old user ID, new user ID, and migration time. The primary key is the migration ID, and there are foreign key constraints on the old and new user IDs that reference the \"user\" table.\n7. \"session\" - This table stores information about sessions, including their session ID, consumer ID, expiration time, MFA status, signature, and issuer. The primary key is the session ID, and there is a foreign key constraint on the consumer ID that references the \"authenticated_consumer\" table.\n8. \"encrypted_data_test\" - This table stores information about encrypted data, including their data ID, regular data, sensitive data 1, sensitive data 2, signature, and issuer. The primary key is the data ID.\nEach table has its own set of columns, with some columns having specific data types, constraints, and relationships with other tables. For example, the \"user\" table has a primary key on the user ID, and the \"authenticated_user\" table has a foreign key constraint on the user ID that references the \"user\" table. The \"service\" table has a primary key on the service ID, and the \"worker_node\" table has a foreign key constraint on the service ID that references the \"service\" table. The \"authenticated_consumer\" table has a primary key on the user ID and consumer ID, and the \"user_migration\" table has foreign key constraints on the old and new user IDs that reference the \"user\" table. The \"session\" table has a foreign key constraint on the consumer ID that references the \"authenticated_consumer\" table. The \"encrypted_data_test\" table has a primary key on the data ID.",
  "gold_question": "User Authentication and Management System\nThis system is designed to manage and authenticate users while tracking their interactions with various services and worker nodes in the system. The key functionalities of the system are:\n1.User Registration and Management:\nStore user registration information, including username, full name, email, password hash, registration date, and last login date.\nEnsure the uniqueness of usernames and email addresses.\n2.User Authentication:\nStore information of authenticated users, including username, full name, authentication level (ring), and authentication time.\nEnsure the uniqueness of authenticated usernames.\nRecord signatures and issuer information during the authentication process.\n3.User Migration:\nTrack user migrations from an old system to the new system, recording the old and new user IDs, along with the migration time.\n4.Authenticated Consumer Management:\nStore information of authenticated consumers, including name, description, type, and associated user ID.\nStore configuration data,permissions, and signatures for each consumer.\n5.Session Management:\nStore session information, including session ID, associated consumer ID, expiration time, and MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) status.\nRecord session signatures and issuer information.\n6.Service Management:\nStore information of various services in the system, including service name, type, URL, and configuration.\nTrack the last heartbeat time and monitoring status of services.\nRecord service signatures and issuer information.\n7.Worker Node Management:\nStore information of worker nodes, including node name, status, associated service ID, operating system, architecture, and last heartbeat time.\nRecord worker node signatures and issuer information.\nEach service contains multiple worker nodes, each user may have multiple authenticated consumers, and each consumer will have multiple sessions.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0121.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0121\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `full_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password_hash` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `last_login_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `authenticated_user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `authentication_level` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `authentication_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `authenticated_user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `service` (\n  `service_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `service_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `service_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `service_url` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `configuration_data` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_heartbeat_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `monitoring_status` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`service_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `worker_node` (\n  `node_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `node_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `service_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `operating_system` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `architecture` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_heartbeat_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`node_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `worker_node_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`service_id`) REFERENCES `service` (`service_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `authenticated_consumer` (\n  `consumer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `configuration_data` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `permissions` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`, `consumer_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `authenticated_consumer_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_migration` (\n  `migration_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `old_user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `new_user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `migration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`migration_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_migration_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`old_user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `user_migration_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`new_user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `session` (\n  `session_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `consumer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `mfa_status` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`session_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `session_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`consumer_id`) REFERENCES `authenticated_consumer` (`consumer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `encrypted_data_test` (\n  `data_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `regular_data` text NOT NULL,\n  `sensitive_data_1` text NOT NULL,\n  `sensitive_data_2` text NOT NULL,\n  `signature` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `issuer` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`data_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
